BOTULINUM TOXIN A IN ANTI–GAD-
POSITIVE STIFF-LIMB SYNDROME
Stiff-person syndrome (SPS) is a rare disorder character-
ized by progressive stiffness and rigidity of axial muscles.
Antibodies to glutamic acid decarboxylase (GAD) are
present in the majority of these patients.
1 Typically, clini-
cal symptoms and pathologic electromyographic activity
respond positively to benzodiazepines.
2
Recently, several patients have been described whose
symptoms were strictly conﬁned to 1 lower limb. This
condition was ﬁven the name ‘ ‘stiff-limb syndrome’ ’
(SLS).
3 Generally considered to be a focal form of SPS,
SLS usually has a poorer response to benzodiazepines.
Anti-GAD antibodies are less common in SLS than in
SPS: 15% vs. 88%, respectively.
3 Comprehensive treat-
ment recommendations are not available, as clinical tri-
als are hampered by the rarity of the syndrome.
3
<seg id="s0001" group="l0001" targets="p1" role="patient_specific">We report the case of a 40-year-old woman who pre-
sented with a 9-year history of progressive left leg stiff-
ness. Initially she had difﬁculty walking at higher speeds,
and gradually developed marked rigidity in her right leg
and held it extended at the knee. Painful knee-extension
spasms were superimposed on submaximal extension
stiffness with emotional stimuli. Repeated magnetic reso-
nance imaging of the entire spinal cord and the brain
were normal. Routine laboratory tests were unremark-
able, apart from mildly elevated glucose levels. Immuno-
logical parameters were also negative. Four years after
the onset of symptoms she was tested for anti-GAD anti-
bodies and found to be positive, with a value of >500
U/ml (normal <7 U/ml).
Based on the clinical picture, the lack of symptom
spread over the years and the increased anti-GAD
